    "question": {
      ":question/id": "72d1ef7f-e793-5d41-b0a8-4e609b48c9bf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Let $R=(1,6)\\times(0,4)$ and define\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}e^{x + y}&1<x<6,\\ 0<y<1\\\\x^{2} - y^{2}&1<x<6,\\ 1<y<4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSet $f=0$ on the dividing line. Which sum gives $\\iint_R f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ by additivity? You do not need to evaluate the integrals.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Read the two coordinate intervals in each condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_1&=(1,6)\\times(0,1)\\\\R_2&=(1,6)\\times(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe interiors do not overlap. The dividing line has area zero. Match each formula to its own rectangle and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\iint_{(1,6)\\times(0,1)}(e^{x + y})\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&+\\iint_{(1,6)\\times(1,4)}(x^{2} - y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$",
